00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:06.309 align:start position:0%
astronomers believe that every century a  few stars in the milky way explode as  supernovae blasting out a brightly  glowing cloud of gas and dust and firing  particles in all directions  most of these events are too far from  earth to impact us but if one happened  nearby the effect could be dramatic  new evidence suggests that in the past  few million years at least two  supernovae occurred close to earth  dusting it with radioactive nuclei  the question is were they near enough to  impact life on our planet  astronomers now hypothesize that several  million years ago a barrage of  supernova-generated particles known as  cosmic rays could have depleted the  ozone layer increased cancer rates  sparked wildfires and even started an  ice age  most supernovae occur when stars run out  of fuel and collapse under their own  gravity the shock of the implosion  blasts debris far into space some of  that debris eventually reaches  neighboring star systems and settles on  planets  among this material are radioactive  isotopes like iron 60 with half-lives of  millions of years too young to have been  there since the earth formed 4.5 billion  years ago  scientists have now found traces of iron  60 in ocean crusts antarctic snow and  even in lunar soil all deposited from  far away stars  these deposits suggest local supernovae  occurred 2.5 and 6 million years ago  the dusting of atoms would not be  noticed by any inhabitants of earth but  supernovae have other effects the  initial blast full of x-rays and gamma  rays could be lethal at very close range  but even from a few hundred light years  away a supernova can still pack a slow  burning punch  some scientists think the blast 2.5  million years ago was close enough to  have substantially changed the evolution  of our planet  supernovae produced cosmic rays  particles like protons accelerated to  near light speed which could have  wreaked havoc on earth's biosphere  the rays from the most recent supernova  may have depleted the earth's ozone  layer letting in more of the sun's  ultraviolet light  reactions in the stratosphere may have  increased fertilizing nitrogen oxide  compounds leading to a surge in plant  growth that could have cooled the  climate helping to initiate an ice age  and animals may have felt the  supernova's effects directly when cosmic  rays hit the upper atmosphere they  generate showers of particles including  muons heavy short-lived cousins of  electrons that can penetrate deep into  animal tissue  organisms on earth would have received  triple the normal dose of radiation from  these muons increasing their risk of  cancer  these showers of particles would also  leave tracks of ionized air molecules as  they streak through the atmosphere  creating pathways for lightning that may  have sparked wildfires across the globe  finding the real culprits for the recent  supernovae is impossible as they fade to  near invisibility after tens of  thousands of years  but astronomers have tracked a group of  nearby stars a few dozen in total and  wound back the clock to see where they  were 2.5 million years ago  sure enough they swung within 300 light  years of earth at the time from the ages  of surviving members of the group it's  likely some would have been ripe for  explosion just the right place in time  to dust earth with atoms and maybe do  more damage  the exact effects of supernovae on earth  remain speculative  some scientists suggest the blast caused  extinctions while others think those  events had unrelated causes  for now more research is needed to  determine how supernovae may have  changed earth's timeline  so next time you look up at the stars be  thankful you weren't alive 2.5 million  years ago when a supernova lit up the  sky  [Music]
 

